    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d608">

                <pb facs="0624" n="612" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg612"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.00B International Red Day/220: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-05-03T14:00:00+03:00">May 3, 1941—2
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received May 3—1:56 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>897. The press accounts of the May Day parades throughout the Soviet Union
                    emphasize the new Soviet artillery and aircraft. The usual figures of the
                    numbers of airplanes taking part in the demonstrations are omitted.
                        Timoshenko’s<anchor n="41" xml:id="d608-f1"/> speech in the Red Square,
                    which is published today, emphasized the desire of the Soviet Government for
                    peace and for the improvement of its relations with all nations that likewise
                    desire better relations. Minister of War called for <pb facs="0625" n="613"
                        xml:id="pg613"/> vigilance and the continued strengthening of Soviet
                    economic and military power.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d608-f1" n="41" xml:id="d608-f1-content">
                <p>Marshal Semën Konstantinovich Timoshenko, People’s Commissar for Defense of the
                    Soviet Union since May 1940.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
